inject(HttpClient) as a field initialiser constructor(private http: HttpClient) export class UserPanel {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
} export class UserPanel {
  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}
} Works in functions and field initialisers, not just constructors — which is what makes functional guards, functional interceptors and reusable composables possible at all. It also drops the constructor boilerplate and plays better with inheritance.

Standalone by default — no `standalone: true`, no NgModule standalone: true written explicitly (v15–18), or declared in an NgModule (pre-v15) @Component({
  selector: 'user-panel',
  imports: [DatePipe],
  template: `{{ joined | date }}`,
})
export class UserPanel {} @Component({
  selector: 'user-panel',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [DatePipe],
  template: `{{ joined | date }}`,
})
export class UserPanel {} NgModules existed to tell the compiler what a template could see. Standalone components carry their own `imports` array, so the indirection was pure overhead. v14 previewed it, v15 stabilised it, v19 made it the default and made the flag redundant.

@if / @for / @switch — built into the template language *ngIf / *ngFor / *ngSwitch structural directives, imported from CommonModule @if (user(); as u) {
  <p>{{ u.name }}</p>
} @else {
  <p>Signed out</p>
}

@for (item of items(); track item.id) {
  <li>{{ item.label }}</li>
} <p *ngIf="user$ | async as u; else signedOut">{{ u.name }}</p>
<ng-template #signedOut><p>Signed out</p></ng-template>

<li *ngFor="let item of items; trackBy: trackById">{{ item.label }}</li> Built-in syntax needs no imports, type-narrows properly inside the block, and lets the compiler generate far less code. @for also *requires* a track expression, which turns the most common Angular performance bug into a compile error.

signal() / computed() / effect() A plain field plus manual change detection, or a BehaviorSubject you had to subscribe to count = signal(0);
double = computed(() => this.count() * 2);

increment() { this.count.update(n => n + 1); } count$ = new BehaviorSubject(0);
double$ = this.count$.pipe(map(n => n * 2));

increment() { this.count$.next(this.count$.value + 1); } Signals track their own readers, so Angular knows exactly which views depend on which state — that is the mechanism zoneless change detection is built on. They arrived in preview in v16 and stabilised in v20.

input() / input.required() — a signal you read with () @Input() decorator on a plain field userId = input.required<string>();
label = input('Untitled');

heading = computed(() => `${this.label()} (${this.userId()})`); @Input({ required: true }) userId!: string;
@Input() label = 'Untitled';

ngOnChanges() { this.heading = `${this.label} (${this.userId})`; } A signal input is readable in computed() and effect() without a setter or ngOnChanges, and input.required() makes a missing input a compile-time error rather than an undefined at runtime.

@Service() @Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' }) @Service()
export class UserStore {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
} @Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class UserStore {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
} Root-provided was what almost every service wanted, so v22 made it the shape of the decorator rather than an option you configure. @Service() is auto-provided by default; pass { autoProvided: false } for a service that must be listed in a providers array, or { factory } to build it from a function.

provideAppInitializer(() => …) { provide: APP_INITIALIZER, useFactory: …, multi: true } bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    provideAppInitializer(() => inject(ConfigService).load()),
  ],
}); bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    {
      provide: APP_INITIALIZER,
      useFactory: (config: ConfigService) => () => config.load(),
      deps: [ConfigService],
      multi: true,
    },
  ],
}); The token form needed multi: true and a factory, and forgetting either failed silently at runtime rather than at compile time. The provide* functions take the callback directly. The same swap applies to ENVIRONMENT_INITIALIZER and PLATFORM_INITIALIZER.

output<T>() @Output() name = new EventEmitter<T>() saved = output<User>();

save() {
  this.saved.emit(this.user());
} @Output() saved = new EventEmitter<User>();

save() {
  this.saved.emit(this.user);
} An output is not a stream you subscribe to, so modelling it as an EventEmitter (an RxJS Subject) always promised more than it delivered. output() exposes only emit(), which is the entire API a component actually needs, and it cannot be accidentally subscribed to or completed.

viewChild() / viewChildren() / contentChild() — signals @ViewChild() / @ContentChild() decorators, with a static flag and a lifecycle hook to wait for chart = viewChild.required<ElementRef>('chart');

constructor() {
  afterNextRender(() => this.draw(this.chart().nativeElement));
} @ViewChild('chart', { static: true }) chart!: ElementRef;

ngAfterViewInit() {
  this.draw(this.chart.nativeElement);
} The decorator form set a plain property at some point during a lifecycle hook, so reading it too early gave you undefined and the fix was to know which hook. A signal query is just a signal: read it whenever, use it in computed(), and the timing question disappears along with the static flag.

@defer with a trigger A route-level lazy load, or a manual dynamic import plus ViewContainerRef @defer (on viewport) {
  <sales-chart [data]="data()" />
} @placeholder {
  <div class="skeleton"></div>
} @loading (minimum 200ms) {
  <spinner />
} // component
async loadChart() {
  const { SalesChart } = await import('./sales-chart');
  this.container.createComponent(SalesChart);
} Lazy loading used to be something you could only do at a routing boundary. @defer moves it into the template, so a heavy chart or a rarely-opened dialog is split out of the bundle without restructuring the application around it. The dependencies used only inside the block go with it automatically.

@let name = expression; *ngIf="expr as name", or a wrapper <ng-container> whose only job was to hold the alias @let user = user$ | async;
@let total = cart().items.reduce((n, i) => n + i.price, 0);

<p>{{ user?.name }} owes {{ total }}</p> <ng-container *ngIf="user$ | async as user">
  <p>{{ user.name }}</p>
</ng-container> Aliasing used to be a side effect of a structural directive, which meant you needed a conditional you did not want in order to name a value you did. Worse, *ngIf's alias silently dropped the block when the value was falsy — 0 and '' vanished. @let just declares a name.

takeUntilDestroyed() takeUntil(this.destroy$) with a manual Subject and ngOnDestroy export class Ticker {
  constructor() {
    interval(1000).pipe(takeUntilDestroyed())
      .subscribe(n => console.log(n));
  }
} export class Ticker implements OnDestroy {
  private destroy$ = new Subject<void>();

  ngOnInit() {
    interval(1000).pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$))
      .subscribe(n => console.log(n));
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    this.destroy$.next();
    this.destroy$.complete();
  }
} No boilerplate Subject, no lifecycle hook to forget. It ties to DestroyRef, so it also works in services and functions — anywhere with an injection context — not just components.

OnPush — applied automatically when changeDetection is not set Default (check-everything) unless you opted into ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush by hand @Component({
  selector: 'user-panel',
  template: `{{ name() }}`,
})
export class UserPanel {} @Component({
  selector: 'user-panel',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
  template: `{{ name() }}`,
})
export class UserPanel {} The old default re-checked every binding in the tree on every event. OnPush was always the right answer for signal-based components, so v22 stopped making you ask for it.

form(model) — the model signal is the form new FormGroup({ … }) — a parallel model you kept in sync by hand import { form, required, email, minLength } from '@angular/forms/signals';

profile = signal({ name: '', email: '' });

profileForm = form(this.profile, (path) => {
  required(path.name);
  minLength(path.name, 2);
  required(path.email);
  email(path.email);
});

// profileForm.name().value() / .valid() / .touched() / .errors() profileForm = new FormGroup({
  name: new FormControl('', [Validators.required, Validators.minLength(2)]),
  email: new FormControl('', [Validators.required, Validators.email]),
});

// this.profileForm.get('name')?.valid   <- 'name' is just a string
// and the real model lives somewhere else entirely A reactive form was a second copy of your data with its own type, its own change stream and its own ideas about validity, and half of form code existed to keep the two in step. form() takes your existing model signal as the source of truth and does not copy it — writing through the field writes the model. Validation is declared against typed paths, so a renamed property is a compile error rather than a string that silently stops matching.

Zoneless — signals tell Angular what changed zone.js patching every async browser API to guess that something might have changed bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideZonelessChangeDetection()],
});

// angular.json / package.json: zone.js is no longer a dependency
// and no longer in "polyfills" bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection({ eventCoalescing: true })],
});

// polyfills: ["zone.js"] zone.js monkey-patched setTimeout, addEventListener, fetch and the rest so Angular could re-check the entire component tree whenever any of them fired. It worked without you thinking about it, at the cost of ~13KB, a patched global environment that confused debuggers and third-party libraries, and change detection runs triggered by events that changed nothing. Signals carry the information directly, so the guessing is unnecessary. Experimental in v18 as provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection, stable without the prefix in v20.2, and the default for new applications from v21.

httpResource() / resource() — value, isLoading and error as signals A subscription in ngOnInit writing to three separate fields, or an Observable plus the async pipe userId = input.required<string>();

user = httpResource<User>(() => `/api/users/${this.userId()}`);

// template:
// @if (user.isLoading()) { <spinner /> }
// @else if (user.error()) { <p>Failed.</p> }
// @else { <p>{{ user.value()?.name }}</p> } user?: User;
loading = false;
error?: unknown;

ngOnChanges() {
  this.loading = true;
  this.http.get<User>(`/api/users/${this.userId}`).subscribe({
    next: (u) => { this.user = u; this.loading = false; },
    error: (e) => { this.error = e; this.loading = false; },
  });
  // nothing cancels the previous request
} Every codebase reinvented the same trio of loading/error/value fields, and every one of them got cancellation slightly wrong — a slow response for user 1 arriving after you had already switched to user 2. A resource re-runs when its reactive request changes and abandons the previous result, so the out-of-order case cannot happen. In preview from v19; stable in v22.

A function, registered with withInterceptors([…]) A class implementing HttpInterceptor, registered against the HTTP_INTERCEPTORS multi token export const authInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) => {
  const token = inject(AuthService).token();
  return next(token ? req.clone({
    setHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
  }) : req);
};

provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([authInterceptor])) @Injectable()
export class AuthInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  constructor(private auth: AuthService) {}
  intercept(req: HttpRequest<unknown>, next: HttpHandler) {
    const token = this.auth.token;
    return next.handle(token ? req.clone({
      setHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
    }) : req);
  }
}

{ provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS, useClass: AuthInterceptor, multi: true } The class form needed a DI registration with multi: true that was easy to get wrong and failed silently when you did. A functional interceptor is a function that calls inject() for what it needs — and because ordering is the array's order, the sequence is visible at the call site instead of depending on provider registration order.

A function typed CanActivateFn A class implementing the CanActivate interface export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = (route, state) => {
  const auth = inject(AuthService);
  const router = inject(Router);
  return auth.isLoggedIn() || router.createUrlTree(['/login']);
};

{ path: 'admin', canActivate: [authGuard], loadComponent: () => import('./admin').then(m => m.Admin) } @Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class AuthGuard implements CanActivate {
  constructor(private auth: AuthService, private router: Router) {}
  canActivate() {
    return this.auth.isLoggedIn() || this.router.createUrlTree(['/login']);
  }
}

{ path: 'admin', canActivate: [AuthGuard], component: AdminComponent } A guard is one decision, so a whole class with a constructor to get its dependencies was ceremony. The class-based interfaces were deprecated in v15 and removed in v16 — this is one of the few Angular changes with a hard deadline attached, so code still using them will not compile on a supported version.

A component input, bound automatically by the router Subscribing to ActivatedRoute.paramMap provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding())

// component — no router dependency at all
id = input.required<string>();
user = httpResource<User>(() => `/api/users/${this.id()}`); constructor(private route: ActivatedRoute) {}

ngOnInit() {
  this.route.paramMap.pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$)).subscribe(params => {
    this.id = params.get('id')!;
    this.load();
  });
} A route parameter is data arriving from outside the component, which is exactly what an input is. With withComponentInputBinding() the router binds params, query params, data and resolved values straight to matching inputs — so the component stops depending on the router at all, which also makes it trivial to test.

Vitest via the @angular/build:unit-test builder Karma + Jasmine via @angular-devkit/build-angular:karma // angular.json
"test": {
  "builder": "@angular/build:unit-test"
} // angular.json
"test": {
  "builder": "@angular-devkit/build-angular:karma",
  "options": { "karmaConfig": "karma.conf.js" }
} Karma drives a real browser per run and predates the Vite-based build pipeline Angular now uses; Vitest reuses that pipeline, so tests start in a fraction of the time. v21 shipped the unified builder and made Vitest the default for new projects. Karma still runs — the same builder accepts runner: 'karma' — but is deprecated and takes no new features. Note that the schematic converts your Jasmine *assertions*; switching the runner itself is a one-line change to angular.json.

retry({ count, delay }) retryWhen(errors => errors.pipe(delay(1000))) source.pipe(
  retry({ count: 3, delay: 1000 })
) source.pipe(
  retryWhen(errors => errors.pipe(
    delay(1000),
    take(3),
  ))
) retryWhen exposed a notifier Observable that almost everybody used incorrectly — completing it silently stopped retrying, and errors in the notifier were easy to swallow. The config object covers the real use cases directly.

source.pipe(map(…), filter(…)) source.map(…).filter(…), after importing 'rxjs/add/operator/map' import { map, filter } from 'rxjs/operators';

source.pipe(
  filter(x => x > 0),
  map(x => x * 2),
); import 'rxjs/add/operator/map';
import 'rxjs/add/operator/filter';

source
  .filter(x => x > 0)
  .map(x => x * 2); Patching operators onto Observable.prototype meant one import anywhere in the application changed the type for everyone — and made the library impossible to tree-shake, so you shipped all of RxJS whether you used three operators or ninety. Pipeable operators are plain functions: importable, tree-shakeable, and testable on their own.

map(() => value) and switchMap(() => obs) mapTo(value) and switchMapTo(obs) clicks.pipe(map(() => 'clicked'));
clicks.pipe(switchMap(() => this.load())); clicks.pipe(mapTo('clicked'));
clicks.pipe(switchMapTo(this.load())); // load() ran once, up front The *To variants saved a few characters and doubled the API surface, and people reached for them without noticing that the argument is evaluated eagerly — switchMapTo(this.load()) calls load() once, at pipe-construction time, not per emission. Removing them removes that trap.

toSignal(obs$) — read it like any other signal The async pipe, or a manual subscribe into a field user = toSignal(this.user$, { initialValue: null });

// template: {{ user()?.name }}
// and in code: this.user()?.name user$ = this.service.user$;

// template: {{ (user$ | async)?.name }}
// in code: not available without subscribing again The async pipe works, but a value read three times in a template means three subscriptions unless you remember to share, and it cannot be read from component code at all. toSignal subscribes once, unsubscribes with the injection context, and gives you a plain signal usable everywhere — including inside computed().

The projects option inside the root config A separate vitest.workspace.ts file // vitest.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    projects: ['packages/*'],
  },
}) // vitest.workspace.ts
export default ['packages/*'] Two files described one thing. Folding projects into the root config removed a whole category of "which file wins?" confusion. Deprecated in 3.2, gone in 4.

Blocked by default; opt packages in explicitly Every dependency's preinstall/install/postinstall ran automatically # pnpm 11 — pnpm-workspace.yaml
allowBuilds:
  - esbuild
  - fsevents // pnpm 10 — package.json
{
  "pnpm": {
    "onlyBuiltDependencies": ["esbuild", "fsevents"]
  }
} A postinstall script is arbitrary code execution at install time, and the Rspack supply-chain attack used exactly that to ship a cryptominer. pnpm 10 turned it off by default. In v10 you allow-listed with pnpm.onlyBuiltDependencies; v11 removed that whole family — onlyBuiltDependencies, onlyBuiltDependenciesFile, neverBuiltDependencies, ignoredBuiltDependencies and ignoreDepScripts are all gone — and replaced them with a single allowBuilds list. v11 also defaults strictDepBuilds to true, so a package needing a build that you have not allowed is an error rather than a warning.

pnpm-workspace.yaml (per project) and ~/.config/pnpm/config.yaml (global) The pnpm field in package.json, and .npmrc # pnpm-workspace.yaml
packages:
  - 'packages/*'
allowBuilds:
  - esbuild // package.json
{
  "pnpm": {
    "onlyBuiltDependencies": ["esbuild"]
  }
}

# .npmrc
auto-install-peers=true pnpm's settings had been squatting in two files owned by other tools. v11 split them cleanly: .npmrc keeps registry and auth settings, everything pnpm-specific moves to YAML. Environment variables moved too — npm_config_* is no longer read, use pnpm_config_*.
